ghan man and his little boy have  been reunited in canada after spending  months apart  the two were united monday at toronto's  pearson airport the toddler was  separated from his family during the  mayhem at kabul airport last month turns  out he'd been put on a plane to qatar  and remained there until officials were  able to track him down speaking through  an interpreter the boy's father says the  reunion has been unbelievable  he cannot put his feelings into words  but he was really really happy when he  saw his son  the boy's mother and four siblings  remain in afghanistan but the father is  hopeful that they too will come to  canada  still had accusations of islamophobia on  the campaign trail and the candidate  who's firing back  as we hit the home stretch of this  election campaign we're looking at some  of the first-time candidates and the  backstories behind their political drive  mike armstrong introduces us to ensef  heider the block candidate in sherbrooke  quebec and human rights activist whose  husband is imprisoned in saudi arabia  the public part of politics isn't new  for ensof hyder  for years she's toured the world  speaking about her husband's  imprisonment calling for his release  freedom of expression is one of the  things she wants to push in parliament  if i have a voice in the house of  commons in ottawa she says i will use  that voice  now it was her husband's voice that  landed him in prison rayef badawi a  writer in saudi arabia was arrested in  2012. for the crime of insulting islam  he was sentenced to 10 years and a  thousand lashes  this was badawi in 2015 getting the  first 50 lashes  hydra says seeing that video was hard  but that she was less concerned about  the physical pain her husband was  feeling than the mental toll of his  imprisonment  hydra moved to canada as a refugee with  the couple's three children they've  lived in sherbert quebec since 2013.  she says she's been asked often why  someone who's been in canada for less  than a decade and a citizen for barely  three years would run for the black  quebecois a party that's working to  break up the country  heider says she is thankful to canada  but that she found her independence in  quebec and feels quebec should find its  independence  i'm a citizen like everyone else she  says i have the right to choose  heider has come under heavy criticism  for getting too close to the far right  she's been accused of being islamophobic  she supported maxim bernier's people's  party of canada before the last election  in 2019 she met with prominent  anti-muslim activist robert spencer  calling him a good friend and earlier  this year tweeted criticism of the cbc  for hiring a journalist who wears a  hijab saying quote it started like that  in iran  islamophobia haider says islamophobia  doesn't exist and as for supporting  controversial figures she argued simply  they were nice to her so she was nice to  them  now there appears to be some discomfort  in the party about some of those  positions campaigning in the riding with  the leader of the block reporters tried  to ask haider questions  didn't let her speak while he was there  saying it wasn't the time but since then  she hasn't held back  mike armstrong global news sherbert  quebec  next the fight to get the highest honor  for a canadian war hero  canada has always put humility before  showmanship when it comes to celebrating  our armed forces  the u.s australia britain and new  zealand all awarded their top military  honors to afghanistan war veterans but  as mike delay explains a group of  canadian veterans including former chief  of the defense staff general rick  hillier is hoping to change that and  soon  the agony of this moment must have been  unbearable for private jess larochelle  saying goodbye to two good friends all  the while in physical agony  you  broke your back yet you still carried  the casket the next day at the ramp  ceremony yeah i was having trouble  walking and that and  the warning officer told me to go to  the hospital and i said after i  carried blake or darcy's casket i'll go  right away private blake williamson and  sergeant darcy tedford were killed by  the taliban securing a road construction  project in afghanistan in 2006.  lara shell survived the attack if only  barely fifteen years later la rochelle's  injuries have caught up with him he  spoke with us from a hospital bed in  north bay about a group of afghanistan  veterans who are trying to right a wrong  la rochelle the group says should have  been awarded the canadian victoria cross  the taliban attack was swift  and he was knocked unconscious by an rpg  when he came to his back was broken as  was his gun and over 20 taliban fighters  were approaching he found a pile of 15  m72 rocket launchers and fired them all  off one by one from what i understand  that his firing with the rockets was so  accurate that he was hitting them  directly and that maybe also indicate  just how close they were and we in my  view would have lost a lot of canadians  that day that we did not lose because of  just  just  larichelle's valor canada hasn't awarded  a victoria cross since 1945. general  rick hillier admits the committee that  decided on medals in afghanistan has  been conservative in its decision-making  so now he along with other veterans have  sent a letter to the governor general  asking for larochelle's case to be  reviewed before his health declines  further things are not looking good for  guests  if you're going to  do something like this and you want  because jess would be extremely  honored  that you know to get it as a alive  don't ask me how he would feel once he's  passed so now the battle is with  government bureaucracy but la rochelle  has faced overwhelming odds before  mike relay global news toronto  and that is it for global national for  this friday night i'm farah nasser thank  you so much for spending part of your  evening with us tonight's your canada is 